Constipation (Opioid-Induced/Other)
Palliative & End of Life
Infrequent hard stools, Crampy lower abdominal pain, Palpable left lower quadrant fecal mass

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-016
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lower abdominal pain and uncontrolled overflow incontinence masking a severe fecal impaction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, formal home care nurse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported severe, diffuse lower abdominal pain increasing progressively over 48 hours, coupled with profound nausea and an inability to tolerate oral intake or scheduled analgesia. The patient is exhibiting painful, completely unproductive straining. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out acute bowel obstruction, physically examine for impaction, and initiate aggressive pharmacological or manual disimpaction prior to the next in-hours period to prevent bowel ischemia or perforation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is cramping in absolute agony. I feel like I constantly need to go, but only liquid leaks out and the pain just gets worse."
History of Present Illness: Over the past week, the patient has had severe, highly infrequent passage strictly of small, hard, and definitively desiccated stools. This progressed to a continuous, unrelenting sensation of incomplete rectal emptying. For the last two days, there has been severe, deep, and diffuse crampy lower abdominal pain progressively worsening. Today, paradoxical, completely uncontrolled liquid perianal leakage strongly surrounding a severe hard impaction began. Straining is strictly and painfully unproductive. The patient has unrelenting, profound subjective severe nausea explicitly secondary to severe bowel distension. Anorexia and an absolute refusal to consume normal dietary intake are evident. There is a clear history of taking continuously escalating doses of strong systemic opioid analgesics for metastatic bone pain.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe abdominal pain; completely reliant on nursing staff for pad changes.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of skin breakdown due to continuous liquid perianal leakage; extreme distress preventing rest.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 20 bpm | SpO2: 97% on room air | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient is in visible distress, grimacing, diaphoresic, and guarding the abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals generalized distension. A hard, explicitly palpable, distinctly large fecal mass is definitively felt deep within the left lower abdominal quadrant. Bowel sounds are hyperactive proximally and sluggish distally. Digital rectal examination (DRE) confirms a rock-hard fecal impaction in the rectal vault with liquid stool bypassing it. The diagnostic tetrad is confirmed by the history of infrequent hard stools, the active crampy lower abdominal pain, and the palpable left lower quadrant fecal mass confirmed both abdominally and via DRE.
Environmental Observations: Incontinence pads are frequently soiled with small amounts of brown liquid. Room temperature is adequate.
Medication Reconciliation: The patient has been taking high-dose sustained-release oxycodone without an accompanying prophylactic stimulant laxative.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Constipation (Opioid-Induced/Other) - Status: Exacerbated (Severe Impaction)
Differentials:

Malignant mechanical bowel obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of liquid overflow stool, absence of feculent emesis, and the physical confirmation of rock-hard stool in the rectal vault driving the blockage.

Acute appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the pain localizing entirely to the left lower quadrant and rectum, rather than the right lower quadrant, and absence of fever.

Urinary retention - Ruled out/unlikely due to a non-palpable bladder and the patient passing adequate volumes of clear urine independently of the fecal leakage.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering an acute crisis of severe opioid-induced constipation culminating in a massive fecal impaction and painful overflow incontinence. The objective finding of a rock-hard palpable mass in the left lower quadrant and rectal vault directly explains the agonizing crampy pain, unrelenting nausea, and paradoxical liquid leakage. Delaying medical assessment until standard hours would subject the patient to agonizing, non-productive tenesmus, severe sleep deprivation, and a high risk of stercoral ulceration or bowel perforation. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to definitively diagnose the impaction via DRE, administer potent rectal interventions to break up the mass, and adjust the deeply flawed oral laxative regimen.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a stat phosphate enema followed by manual fragmentation of the low rectal impaction. Prescribed a continuous regimen of macrogol (osmotic) and senna (stimulant) to commence immediately once the rectal vault is cleared. Administered stat subcutaneous metoclopramide for the nausea.
Positive Behavioral Change: When taking your prescribed opioid pain medication (trigger), immediately take the prescribed stimulant laxative concurrently (behavior) to ensure the bowel keeps moving and prevents the stool from turning into concrete (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Handover to the community nursing team to administer a follow-up micro-enema tomorrow morning if no further result.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensure an adequate supply of barrier cream to protect the perianal skin from the liquid overflow.
Follow-up: GP review in 48 hours to titrate the new dual-laxative regimen.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops sudden, board-like abdominal rigidity, intractable vomiting that becomes dark or foul-smelling, or develops a high fever, the nurse must call 000 immediately for suspected bowel perforation.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The liquid leakage is not diarrhea; it is overflow from a severe blockage. Anti-diarrheal medications must be strictly avoided.
Clinical Course: Opioids will permanently slow the bowel. A daily, aggressive laxative regimen is non-negotiable for the duration of opioid therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not take any medications intended to stop diarrhea. Drink as much water as physically tolerable over the next 24 hours to help the laxatives work.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The strong pain pills paralyzed your bowel, causing the stool to turn into a rock-hard blockage. The liquid leaking out is just slipping past the blockage. The enema was required to break up the rock from the bottom, and the new daily pills will push things from the top to prevent this from ever happening again.
Actionable Steps: Take the new laxative pills every single day, exactly as prescribed, even if you feel you do not need them that day. Apply barrier cream thickly to the skin after every pad change.

